.TH CHTTPSVR_REQ_QUERY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_req_query \- look up all values for a query parameter
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

const char **chttpsvr_req_query(chttpsvr_req *req, const char *key,
                                 size_t *count_out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Parses the query string lazily on the first call, then returns a
NULL-terminated array of every URL-decoded value that belongs to key.
Each key of the query string is URL-decoded and compared with key as
given, as url.Values of the Go net/url package does, so pass the
decoded form: "a b" finds both a+b and a%20b, while "a%20b" finds
neither.  The count is stored in count_out when count_out is not
NULL.  The function returns NULL when the key is absent.

The value strings that the array elements point to stay valid for the
duration of the handler call, but the array itself is a scratch buffer
for this one request that every call to chttpsvr_req_query reuses, so
copy out the pointers you need before you make another call to
chttpsvr_req_query on the same request.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
This function cannot distinguish an out-of-memory failure, which can
happen while the query string is parsed or while the result array
grows, from a genuine "key is absent" result: both give NULL and a
count of 0.  After a NULL return, call chttpsvr_req_query_oom(req) to
tell the two cases apart, or use chttpsvr_req_query_one for a key that
has one value, since that function returns ccol_not_enough_memory when
it runs out of memory.

A query-string component that has a "=" but an empty key half, for
example "?=hello", is a real pair that you can look up: the key ""
maps to the value "hello".  A completely empty component is different:
the parser skips it outright instead of reading it as a false entry
with an empty key and an empty value.  A component is completely empty
when there is nothing at all between two "&" characters, before a
leading "&", or after a trailing "&", as in "?a=1&&b=2" and "?&a=1".
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_req_query_one (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_query_oom (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_raw_query (3)
